.TH m4 "" "Command"
.SH NAME
m4 \- macro processor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBm4 [\fIfile ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
m4 -- macro processor
Usage:	m4 [ file ... ]
If 'file' is '-' or omitted, m4 reads the standard input.
.HE
.B m4
copies each named
.I file
in turn to the standard output, expanding macros as it goes; a
.I file
named
.B \-
means the standard input, and if no
.I file
is given at all,
.B m4
reads the standard input.
.PP
.B m4
copies its input to the output until it finds a potential macro: a
string of letters, digits, and underscores that begins with a
non-digit and is surrounded by non-alphanumerics.
If the string names a defined macro,
.B m4
removes it (and, if the next character is
.BR "(" ,
a parenthesized, comma-separated argument list, collected by
processing it exactly like other text so that an argument may itself
contain macro calls) from the input, and replaces it with the macro's
value.
Up to nine arguments are kept per call; commas inside quotes or
balanced parentheses do not separate arguments, and leading unquoted
whitespace in an argument is discarded.
If the string does not name a defined macro, it is copied through
unchanged.
.PP
A pair of quote characters (by default the backquote
.B `
and the apostrophe
.BR ' )
inhibits macro recognition of the text between them; one level of
quotes is stripped away wherever
.B m4
encounters it, and quotes nest.
.PP
A user-defined macro's value is the text of its definition, with each
.BI $ n
(where
.I n
is a digit) replaced by the
.IR n th
argument of the call (argument 0 is the macro name itself); a
.B $
not followed by a digit is passed through as itself.
.PP
.B m4
provides the following built-in macros:
.IP "\fBchangequote[(\fR[\fIopenquote\fR][\fB,\fR[\fIclosequote\fR]]\fB)]\fR"
Change the quote characters to the first character of
.I openquote
and of
.IR closequote .
A missing or empty argument restores that quote character to its
default
(\fB`\fR or \fB'\fR).
Value is null.
.IP "\fBdecr[(\fInumber\fB)]\fR"
Value is
.I number
(default 0) minus one.
.IP "\fBdefine(\fImacro\fB[,\fIdefinition\fB])\fR"
Define or redefine
.I macro
to expand to
.I definition
(default null).
Value is null.
.IP "\fBdivert[(\fIn\fB)]\fR"
Redirect the output to diversion
.I n
(default 0).
Diversion 0 is the standard output; diversions 1 through 9 are held in
temporary files, to be recovered later with
.BR undivert ;
diverting to any other number discards all further output until the
next
.B divert
call.
Value is null.
.IP \fBdivnum\fR
Value is the number of the diversion currently in effect.
.IP \fBdnl\fR
Delete to newline: discard all characters from the input, up to and
including the next newline.
Value is null.
.IP "\fBdumpdef[(\fImacro\fB[,\fI...\fB])]\fR"
Write, directly to the output, the quoted definition of each named
.IR macro ,
or of every defined macro (in internal hash-table order) if none are
named.
Value is null.
.IP "\fBerrprint(\fItext\fB[,\fI...\fB])\fR"
Write each
.I text
argument to standard error.
Value is null.
.IP "\fBeval(\fIexpression\fB)\fR"
Value is the number that results from evaluating
.I expression
as a C-like integer expression, recognizing, from highest to lowest
precedence: parentheses; exponentiation
(\fB^\fR or \fB**\fR);
unary
\fB+\fR and \fB\-\fR;
\fB*\fR, \fB/\fR, and \fB%\fR;
binary
\fB+\fR and \fB\-\fR;
the relations
\fB==\fR, \fB!=\fR, \fB<=\fR, \fB>=\fR, \fB<\fR, and \fB>\fR;
logical
.B &&
or
.B &
(identical); and logical
.B ||
or
.B |
(identical).
If
.I expression
is omitted, value is 0.
Arithmetic is done in C
.BR long s.
.IP "\fBifdef(\fImacro\fB,\fIdefvalue\fB[,\fIundefvalue\fB])\fR"
Value is
.I defvalue
if
.I macro
is currently defined, or
.I undefvalue
(default null) if not.
.IP "\fBifelse(\fIarg1\fB,\fIarg2\fB,\fIarg3\fB[,\fIarg4\fB,\fIarg5\fB,\fIarg6\fB[,\fIarg7\fB,\fIarg8\fB,\fIarg9\fB]])\fR"
Compare
.I arg1
against
.IR arg2 :
if they are the same string, value is
.IR arg3 ;
otherwise the comparison repeats with the next group of three
arguments, up to three groups (nine arguments) in all.
.IP "\fBinclude(\fIfile\fB)\fR"
Process the contents of
.I file
as further input, as if it had been inserted at this point; a fatal
error results if
.I file
cannot be opened.
.IP "\fBincr[(\fInumber\fB)]\fR"
Value is
.I number
(default 0) plus one.
.IP "\fBindex(\fItext\fB,\fIpattern\fB)\fR"
Value is the character position (counting from 0) of the first
occurrence of
.I pattern
in
.IR text ,
or \-1 if it does not occur (or if either argument is missing).
.IP "\fBlen(\fItext\fB)\fR"
Value is the length of
.I text
(0 if omitted).
.IP "\fBmaketemp(\fIfilenameXXXXXX\fB)\fR"
Value is
.I filenameXXXXXX
with the trailing run of
.BR X 's
replaced by a unique string, as with the system call
.BR mktemp() ;
the argument must be at least six characters long.
.IP "\fBsinclude(\fIfile\fB)\fR"
Like
.BR include ,
but does nothing (instead of failing) if
.I file
cannot be opened.
.IP "\fBsubstr(\fItext\fB[,\fIstart\fB[,\fIcount\fB]])\fR"
Value is the substring of
.I text
beginning at position
.I start
(counting from 0; a negative value counts back from the end) and
running for
.I count
characters (a negative value takes characters before
.IR start ;
if
.I count
is omitted, the rest of
.I text
is taken in the given direction).
.IP "\fBsyscmd(\fIcommand\fB)\fR"
Pass
.I command
to the shell for execution, as with the system call
.BR system() .
Value is null.
.IP "\fBtranslit(\fItext\fB,\fIcharacters\fB[,\fIreplacements\fB])\fR"
Value is
.I text
with each character found in
.I characters
replaced by the character at the same position in
.IR replacements ,
or deleted if
.I replacements
is shorter than
.I characters
(or omitted).
.IP "\fBundefine(\fImacro\fB)\fR"
Remove the definition of
.IR macro ;
a later use of the name is copied through literally.
.IP "\fBundivert[(\fIn\fB[,\fI...\fB])]\fR"
Copy the saved text of diversion
.I n
(1 through 9) into the current output, then discard it; a diversion
that is still the current output is left untouched.
With no arguments, all nine diversions are copied, in numeric order.
Value is null.
.PP
At the end of processing all input files,
.B m4
automatically diverts back to the standard output and undiverts every
remaining diversion.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B cpp
